bout one of its legs, we need to consider the two possible rotations: rotating about the 8 cm leg and rotating about the 15 cm leg. The resulting solids will be cones, and the volume of a cone is given by the formula:\n\n\\[\nV = \\frac{1}{3} \\pi r^2 h\n\\]\n\nwhere \\( r \\) is the radius of the base and \\( h \\) is the height of the cone.\n\n### Case 1: Rotating about the 8 cm leg\n- The 8 cm leg becomes the height \\( h \\) of the cone.\n- The 15 cm leg becomes the radius \\( r \\) of the base of the cone.\n\nSubstituting these values into the volume formula, we get:\n\n\\[\nV_1 = \\frac{1}{3} \\pi (15)^2 (8) = \\frac{1}{3} \\pi (225) (8) = \\frac{1800}{3} \\pi = 600 \\pi\n\\]\n\n### Case 2: Rotating about the 15 cm leg\n- The 15 cm leg becomes the height \\( h \\) of the cone.\n- The 8 cm leg becomes the radius \\( r \\) of the base of the cone.\n\nSubstituting these values into the volume formula, we get:\n\n\\[\nV_2 = \\frac{1}{3} \\pi (8)^2 (15) = \\frac{1}{3} \\pi (64) (15) = \\frac{960}{3} \\pi = 320 \\pi\n\\]\n\nComparing the two volumes, \\( V_1 = 600 \\pi \\) and \\( V_2 = 320 \\pi \\), the maximum possible volume is \\( 600 \\pi \\).\n\nThus, the maximum possible volume of the resulting solid is \\(\\boxed{600\\pi}\\)."},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{600\\pi}","correct":true,"format":"boxed","parseable":true},"id":"geometry:211"}
